## 6. Enforce authorization in storage queries

Expose only scoped reads to the UI and model. Keep unscoped forms such as `searchAll()`, `getVersion(id)`, and `getBlob(hash)` private. Storage reads of sources, originals, index entries, segments, citations, and usage records require a valid `AuthorizationPlan` issued by the policy owner. Verify the plan and its revision independently of caller-provided values.

```text
allowedVersions = requested pinned versions
  JOIN currently effective source access scope
  JOIN explicit cross-project selections and active, unrevoked grants when another project owns the source
  JOIN current source lifecycle eligibility for this purpose

if purpose == sop_context:
  allowedSegments = allowedVersions
    JOIN this project's accepted Adoption for the exact versionId
    JOIN the segmentId and active applicability allowed by Adoption
else:
  allowedSegments = readable segments within allowedVersions

index query = IndexEntry JOIN allowedSegments before matching, scoring, counting, sorting, or paging
original or citation read = object JOIN allowedVersions/allowedSegments before reading
```

List unpublished candidate cards through `allowedSources`. Return authorized source metadata only. Read originals and segments through their separate contracts. Require explicit cross-project selection and effective permission even for candidate metadata.

Filter authorization before reading or scoring. If a semantic index cannot restrict candidates before searching, isolate indexes by scope or first obtain the authorized candidates. Keep post-search UI filtering out of the authorization decision.

Resolve authority and read through a consistent policy revision. If revocation or deletion races the read, return `STALE_POLICY` and recalculate. Revalidate policy revision, citations, and `contextHash` after context assembly and before model egress. The network adapter must consume a valid egress receipt. Its implementation is outside this design.

Apply scope to queries, paging, diagnostics, and caches. Include principal, request project, purpose, explicit versions, cross-project selections, permission and adoption revisions, index generation, and query configuration in the cache key. Invalidate affected entries on revocation. Diagnostic IDs point to local records. Preserve privacy in user-visible errors.

## 7. Deduplication, updates, deletion, and recovery

| Situation | Handling |
| --- | --- |
| Exact original and parse fingerprint for the same source and scope | Return the existing version and idempotent receipt. Record the duplicate import. Keep confirmation tied to the original action. |
| Identical content imported into projects A and B | Reuse immutable original or text blocks internally where applicable. Retain separate sources, versions, index records, adoptions, and grants. Scope deduplication lookups so other projects' possession remains private. |
| Source title or acquisition time changes only | Record a metadata revision. Keep the labels and evidence on older citations. Classification and source content have separate contracts. |
| Content, parser, or normalization changes | Create a version and retain older versions and citations. A new parse fingerprint requires new version identity even with the same original hash. |
| A new version of an adopted source | Start the new version as `pending`. Retain the old adoption state, mark its applicability `review_required`, and record impact. Exclude use pending review from new SOP context by default. Obtain an explicit decision to retain the pinned older version or adopt the new one. |
| Citation to an old version | Historical SOPs and snapshots retain their citation and `superseded/review_required` status. A review can restore current applicability for the older pinned version. Preserve the original excerpt binding. |
| Candidate rejection | Record `rejected` and the reason. Avoid repeated automatic recommendations. A new review decision preserves the old rejection. |
| Withdrawal of adoption or cross-project grant | Block future formal context and flag affected usage. Preserve original confirmations and historical documents and display the citation's actual status. |
| Tombstone | Block new retrieval and model context. Keep the minimal source, version, citation relationship, and deletion event. Show deletion on historical documents and verify recovery permission. |
| Restore a tombstone | Restore readable state when the original remains and hash and locators verify. Recheck permissions and adoption applicability. Keep revocations and withdrawals effective. |
| Physical purge | Use an explicit separate operation. Report affected citations and history, then apply retention rules. Consider all shared deduplication references before clearing blocks. |
| Recovery after purge | Return `purged` when the original is unavailable. Verify old citations after reimport only if all content, parser, and locator hashes match. Otherwise create a new version and rebind through the applicable decision. |

Reclaim physical blocks based on references from all effective and retained records. A broken external URL and local deletion have distinct meanings. Cite a retained, verified local version with its acquisition date and current URL status. Establish current external content separately.

## 8. Jobs, cancellation, recovery, and idempotency

```ts
interface Job {
  jobId: JobId;
  projectId: ProjectId;
  kind: "import" | "parse" | "index" | "impact_review";
  state: "queued" | "running" | "blocked_input" | "cancel_requested"
    | "cancelled" | "failed" | "succeeded";
  inputHash: Hash;
  configFingerprint: string;
  idempotencyKey: string;
  checkpoint?: { stage: string; artifactHashes: Hash[]; lastCommittedRevision: Revision };
  attempt: number;
  error?: KnowledgeError;
  completionReceipt?: {
    versionId?: VersionId;
    adoptionId?: AdoptionId;
    publication: "not_published" | "published";
    adoption: "not_requested" | "accepted" | "failed" | "stale_intent";
  }; // Final asynchronous receipt. Preserve error on partial completion.
}
```

| Rule | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| Idempotency scope | Isolate keys by principal, project or target scope, and command type. The same key and request replay the same receipt. A different request hash returns `IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT`. |
| Repeated submission | Updating a source requires its expected revision. A concurrent publisher that loses the race receives `REVISION_CONFLICT`. Preserve the newer version. |
| Cancellation | `cancel_requested` records a request. Mark `cancelled` after module acknowledgement. Disable staging artifacts before commit. If commit already completed, report actual completion. |
| Interrupted recovery | Check original and artifact hashes, parser configuration, target scope, permissions, revision, and checkpoint. Resume from a reliable checkpoint or explicitly rerun the stage. |
| Authorization on resume | Obtain current authorization. A revoked grant stops the job with an authorization error. |
| Index failure | Retain verifiable originals and versions. Continue serving the versions covered by the previous generation. Report missing coverage and partial results. |
| Retry | Retry retryable errors within a bound. Use the stated recovery path for format, permission, broken citation, and missing input errors. |
| Job diagnostics | Record stage, version, configuration, error, and recovery. Omit full originals and model contexts from logs by default and keep credentials out. |

Accept cancellation signals for reads and context assembly. Prevent model egress after cancellation. Record cancellation, failure, no match, and insufficient source quality distinctly.

## 9. Context and traceable use

1. Retrieve for the actual task and pinned scope. For no match or no effective adoption, return `NO_MATCH` and the current scope. Allow abstention or a request for more evidence. Keep the search within scope.
2. Resolve a complete citation for each selected fragment. Check hashes, range, adoption, and applicability. Include conflicts, assumptions, model suggestions, and stale or unverified conditions with the fragment.
3. Create a local `KnowledgeSnapshot` with pinned versions, citations, adoption revisions, and authorization basis. Use its hash for integrity. Verify approval and authority separately.
4. Reserve budget for task instructions, citation metadata, and output. Trim the least relevant complete units of fragment, citation, and required context first. If shortening a fragment, recalculate the excerpt range and hash and create an exact sub-citation. Preserve citations, negation, and table context.
5. Return `CONTEXT_BUDGET_TOO_SMALL` if even one complete unit cannot fit. List omitted citation IDs and reasons. Keep uncited text out of the remaining space.
6. Project only needed citation IDs, public source labels, version and hash summaries, and page, heading and excerpt ranges for the model. Keep full local locators, other project catalogs, and unrelated content local.
7. Verify before sending. Record the hash of the content actually sent, budget, snapshot, and citations. Keep the receipt locally as evidence of material use. Check citation and answer correctness separately.

Validate output citation IDs against the actual snapshot and verify each claim against the corresponding excerpt. Flag incorrect citations, claims beyond the excerpt, and new suggestions for review. Track formal SOP confirmation by version. Recheck current permission before reproducing an earlier send. Viewing historical material leaves egress inactive.

